*(
    title: Roots of Unity
    description: Why there are exactly n complex numbers whose nth power is one, why they sit at the corners of a regular polygon, and why they add to nothing.
    tags: complex numbers, roots of unity, de moivre, polynomial equations, extension 2
    lesson
)

@include(src: ../preface.part.chalk)

scene{
    Over the real numbers, $x^3 = 1$ has one solution. Cubing is increasing on the reals, so it passes through $1$ once and never returns.

    ===

    Over the complex numbers it has three, and the canvas shows where the other two went. All three sit on the unit circle at $120^\circ$ apart: $1$ itself, then $\omega = -\frac{1}{2} + \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}i$, then $\omega^2 = -\frac{1}{2} - \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}i$.

    definition{
        A complex number satisfying $z^n = 1$ for a given positive integer $n$. For each $n$ there are exactly $n$ of them, and every one has modulus $1$.
    }(
        term: Root of unity
    )

    Checking the middle one is a cube in polar form: $\omega = \operatorname{cis}\frac{2\pi}{3}$, so $\omega^3 = \operatorname{cis}2\pi = 1$. The real solution had not hidden the other two so much as failed to have anywhere to put them — one axis has no room for a point at $120^\circ$.
}[
    canvas.graph{
        circle(x: 0; y: 0; size: 1; colour: grey; !fill)
        point(x: 1; y: 0; colour: orange; label: $1$)

        cue{
            point(x: -0.5; y: 0.866; colour: teal; label: $\omega$)
            point(x: -0.5; y: -0.866; colour: teal; label: $\omega^2$)
        }(
            in: 1
        )
    }(
        x axis label: real axis
        y axis label: imaginary axis
        !show ticks
    )
]

scene{
    # Where the $n$ solutions come from

    Solving $z^n = 1$ is de Moivre's theorem read backwards. Write $z = r\operatorname{cis}\theta$; then $z^n = r^n\operatorname{cis}(n\theta)$, and that has to equal $1 = 1\operatorname{cis}0$.

    ===

    Matching moduli gives $r^n = 1$ with $r$ a non-negative real, so $r = 1$ and every solution lies on the unit circle. Matching arguments is the step that generates the multiplicity: two angles name the same direction when they differ by a whole number of revolutions, so the requirement is not $n\theta = 0$ but $n\theta = 2k\pi$ for some integer $k$.

    ===

    Dividing by $n$ gives $\theta = \frac{2k\pi}{n}$. Running $k$ through $0, 1, \ldots, n - 1$ produces $n$ distinct directions; $k = n$ returns to $\theta = 0$ and everything after that repeats. Hence exactly $n$ roots, no more and no fewer.

    equation{
        z_k = \operatorname{cis}\frac{2k\pi}{n} \qquad k = 0, 1, \ldots, n - 1
    }

    Writing $\omega = \operatorname{cis}\frac{2\pi}{n}$ for the $k = 1$ case makes the whole set $1, \omega, \omega^2, \ldots, \omega^{n-1}$: each root is the previous one turned through another $\frac{2\pi}{n}$.
}[
    #### The equation in polar form

    equation{
        \left(r\operatorname{cis}\theta\right)^{n} = r^{n}\operatorname{cis}(n\theta) = 1\operatorname{cis}0
    }

    cue{
        #### Moduli, then arguments

        equation{
            r^{n} = 1 \implies r = 1
        }

        equation{
            n\theta = 2k\pi, \quad k \in \mathbb{Z}
        }
    }(
        in: 1
    )

    cue{
        #### One angle per $k$, and only $n$ of them

        equation{
            \theta = \frac{2k\pi}{n}, \qquad k = 0, 1, \ldots, n - 1
        }
    }(
        in: 2
    )
]

scene{
    # A regular polygon, and a sum of zero

    Equally spaced points on a circle are the vertices of a regular polygon, so the $n$th roots of unity are the corners of a regular $n$-gon inscribed in the unit circle with one vertex at $1$. The canvas draws the case $n = 6$.

    ===

    That symmetry has an arithmetic consequence: for every $n \ge 2$ the roots sum to zero. With $n = 6$ it can be read straight off the hexagon, since the vertices fall into three pairs pointing in opposite directions. For odd $n$ no such pairing exists and the geometry is less obvious, but the sum still vanishes.

    The general reason is the factorisation in the next section rather than the picture. The picture is worth having anyway, because it is what makes an answer checkable: any solution set to $z^n = 1$ that is not $n$ equally spaced points on the unit circle has an error in it.

    note{
        The same construction solves $z^n = w$ for any non-zero $w$. Its solutions are one $n$th root of $w$ multiplied by each root of unity in turn, so they too are $n$ equally spaced points — on the circle of radius $|w|^{1/n}$, rotated to start at $\frac{\arg w}{n}$.
    }
}[
    canvas.graph{
        circle(x: 0; y: 0; size: 1; colour: grey; !fill)
        point(x: 1; y: 0; colour: orange; label: $1$)
        point(x: 0.5; y: 0.866; colour: teal)
        point(x: -0.5; y: 0.866; colour: teal)
        point(x: -1; y: 0; colour: teal)
        point(x: -0.5; y: -0.866; colour: teal)
        point(x: 0.5; y: -0.866; colour: teal)

        cue.draw{
            polygon(x: 1, 0.5, -0.5, -1, -0.5, 0.5; y: 0, 0.866, 0.866, 0, -0.866, -0.866; colour: teal; !fill)
        }(
            in: 1
            in duration: 1.4s
        )
    }(
        x axis label: real axis
        y axis label: imaginary axis
        !show ticks
    )
]

scene{
    ## Factorising $z^n - 1$

    Every root of unity is a root of the polynomial $z^n - 1$, and there are $n$ of them for a polynomial of degree $n$, so they are all of its roots and it factorises completely over the complex numbers.

    ===

    Dividing out the obvious root $z = 1$ leaves the sum of the remaining powers, and the roots of that quotient are the roots of unity other than $1$. For $n = 3$ this is $z^3 - 1 = (z - 1)(z^2 + z + 1)$, which is why $\omega$ satisfies $\omega^2 + \omega + 1 = 0$ — a relation that collapses most expressions in $\omega$ on sight.

    The vanishing sum falls out of the same factorisation. Expanding $z^n - 1$ as a product of its linear factors, the coefficient of $z^{n-1}$ is minus the sum of the roots; in $z^n - 1$ that coefficient is $0$ for every $n \ge 2$, so the roots sum to zero.

    task{
        Solve $z^4 = -16$, giving the solutions in Cartesian form.
    }[
        $-16 = 16\operatorname{cis}\pi$, so $r = 16^{1/4} = 2$ and $4\theta = \pi + 2k\pi$, giving $\theta = \frac{\pi}{4}, \frac{3\pi}{4}, -\frac{3\pi}{4}, -\frac{\pi}{4}$. In Cartesian form the four solutions are $\sqrt{2} + i\sqrt{2}$, $-\sqrt{2} + i\sqrt{2}$, $-\sqrt{2} - i\sqrt{2}$ and $\sqrt{2} - i\sqrt{2}$ — a square of side $2\sqrt{2}$ centred on the origin.
    ](
        title: Your turn
    )
}[
    #### Complete factorisation

    equation{
        z^{n} - 1 = \prod_{k=0}^{n-1}\left(z - \operatorname{cis}\frac{2k\pi}{n}\right)
    }

    cue{
        #### Dividing out the root $z = 1$

        equation{
            z^{n} - 1 = (z - 1)\left(z^{n-1} + z^{n-2} + \cdots + z + 1\right)
        }

        equation{
            z^{3} - 1 = (z - 1)(z^{2} + z + 1) \implies \omega^{2} + \omega + 1 = 0
        }
    }(
        in: 1
    )
]
